1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,019
Logs can repeat timestamps, messages, and JSON field names

2
00:00:04,135 --> 00:00:05,215
hundreds of times.

3
00:00:05,911 --> 00:00:09,893
EZ PM2 GUI uses a local TSLN based

4
00:00:09,928 --> 00:00:12,924
profile to reduce that repetition in AI prompts.

5
00:00:13,527 --> 00:00:16,349
TSLN means Time Series Lean Notation.

6
00:00:16,859 --> 00:00:20,436
Our profile declares the columns once, then writes one row
per

7
00:00:20,494 --> 00:00:21,318
evidence line.

8
00:00:21,643 --> 00:00:24,929
An equals sign repeats the previous value in the same
column.

9
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:28,272
Every row, stream, and line number remains.

10
00:00:28,783 --> 00:00:32,243
Timestamps stay exact text, and the empty symbol marks an

11
00:00:32,336 --> 00:00:33,648
unparsed timestamp.

12
00:00:34,089 --> 00:00:37,537
Nothing is sorted, grouped, or dropped by compression, so

13
00:00:37,618 --> 00:00:39,302
repeated events remain countable.

14
00:00:39,906 --> 00:00:41,403
The backend redacts first.

15
00:00:41,844 --> 00:00:45,618
It encodes and decodes the candidate, verifies an exact
match,

16
00:00:45,896 --> 00:00:48,358
then compares complete prompt representations.

17
00:00:49,054 --> 00:00:52,456
Compression is selected only for fewer UTF-8 bytes;

18
00:00:52,781 --> 00:00:54,697
otherwise, JSON stays.

19
00:00:55,138 --> 00:00:56,752
Savings depend on the logs.

20
00:00:57,193 --> 00:01:00,119
Fewer bytes do not guarantee equivalent token savings.

21
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:02,348
The captured tail stays bounded.

22
00:01:02,789 --> 00:01:05,935
Saved snapshots and the preview keep their original
redacted

23
00:01:05,993 --> 00:01:06,527
rows.

24
00:01:07,224 --> 00:01:10,811
Open Help and demos for the implementation guide and the
TSLN

25
00:01:10,881 --> 00:01:14,817
paper, published on engrXiv by Chandan Bhagat and Manas

26
00:01:14,875 --> 00:01:15,815
Mudbari.
